{
  "format": "lunelle-patch/1",
  "name": "handpan",
  "about": "A handpan, after recordings of real ones: the note with its octave and its compound fifth tuned true over it, which swell after the strike as the shell hands them the note's energy, the octave at once, the fifth to its loudest at about 170 ms and the double octave at 300. Each is a pair a few cents apart, as a hammered shell's modes are, so it beats slowly, the octave most (±4 dB at about 2 Hz on D3); the note's twin dies first, which is the note's quick fall from the strike before its long ring. The shell's air rings at 95 Hz under every note, 25 to 30 dB down, and the hand lands with a soft thump. A harder strike brings up the overtones",
